 ### Blue Noise Dithering
-A technique that uses a blue noise texture to offset the sampling start point along the ray, reducing banding and layering artifacts caused by low step counts. It is chosen when volumetric layering is prominent and temporal coherence can be maintained via frame-dependent offsets. It differs from UV perturbation by operating in depth space rather than UV space. See [Blue Noise Dithering](791-distance-color-blending.md) for related blending techniques.
+A technique that uses a blue noise texture to offset the sampling start point along the ray, reducing banding and layering artifacts caused by low step counts [S2, unofficial]. It is chosen when volumetric layering is prominent and temporal coherence can be maintained via frame-dependent offsets. It differs from UV perturbation by operating in depth space rather than UV space.
 
 ### Ray Marching Jittering
-A technique that adds random variation to the ray marching step sizes (specifically between 0.5x and 1.5x the fixed step size) to counteract aliasing artifacts in volumes [S3, unofficial]. It is chosen for participating media where fixed stepping causes visible intensity bands. It differs from texture-based dithering by modifying the integration step directly rather than the initial conditions or UVs [S3, unofficial]. See [Ray-marched Shadows](1087-ray-marched-shadows.md) for shadow-specific marching.
+A technique that adds random variation to the ray marching step sizes (specifically between 0.5x and 1.5x the fixed step size) to counteract aliasing artifacts in volumes [S3, unofficial]. It is chosen for participating media where fixed stepping causes visible intensity bands [S3, unofficial]. It differs from texture-based dithering by modifying the integration step directly rather than the initial conditions or UVs [S3, unofficial].
 
